"""Grade 4 life essentials: honesty, big feelings and loss."""
from spec import L, U

E4 = U('Honest, brave and hopeful', 'Morality', 'How do I tell the truth, handle big feelings, and face sadness with hope?', [
L('Telling the truth, even when it is hard',
  'Fourth graders are good at spotting unfairness and quick to excuse their own. The eighth commandment, conscience, and the Church’s teaching on sorry and Confession give a clear way to be honest: say what happened, own it, fix it. The lesson makes honesty practical (homework, games, blaming siblings) and shows that telling the truth frees you.',
  '2464-2470,2475-2487,1776-1778,1424,2409,1807',
  'Exodus 20:16;John 8:32;Luke 19:1-10;Ephesians 4:25',
  'conscience virtue justice reconciliation',
  'Why is it hard to tell the truth when you have made a mistake?',
  'Act out three short “mistake” scenes (a broken thing, a copied answer, a game cheat). For each: act the lie, then the truth, then the fix. Afterwards, write one sentence you can use: “I did it. I am sorry. I will make it right.”',
  'contrition',
  id='4-L1',
  hook='Hide a small object in the room, then act as if you do not know where it is. After a moment, say, “I lied. I hid it.” Ask the class, “How did that feel for you to watch? Why would it feel good to tell the truth?”',
  teach=[
    'The eighth commandment, “You shall not bear false witness,” asks us to tell the truth and be honest in what we say and do. The Catechism says a lie is speaking something false to deceive someone (CCC 2482). Lying hurts trust, and trust is like glue that holds friendships and families together. Even a small lie makes the glue weaker. That is why Jesus said, “Let what you say be simply ‘Yes’ or ‘No’” (Matthew 5:37).',
    'Cheating is acting a lie. Copying homework or breaking a game rule pretends we did something we did not. It hurts us too, because we do not learn, and it is unfair to others. The seventh commandment says that fairness matters in how we win, as well as in what we have (CCC 2409). When you are honest, you can be proud of your own work.',
    'Your conscience is the inner voice that tells you what is right (CCC 1776–1778). Think of it as a small alarm that goes off before and after a wrong choice. We can ignore it, but it is better to listen. We train it by learning what is right, praying, and asking wise people. Telling the truth gets easier each time, the way a muscle grows with use.',
    'Zacchaeus was a tax collector who cheated people. When Jesus came to his house, Zacchaeus stood up and said, “I will give half of my goods to the poor, and if I have defrauded anyone, I will restore it fourfold” (Luke 19:8). Jesus said salvation had come to his house. The way back from a lie is: say what happened, say sorry, and make it right. In Confession we can say sorry to God and receive strength (CCC 1424). Many people find that telling the truth is scary for a minute and a relief for a long time.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What happens inside you when you tell a lie?',
    'Why does Jesus say to let our yes be yes?',
    'What does Zacchaeus teach us about making things right?',
    'Who is a person you trust because they always tell the truth?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is a lie?|Any mistake|Saying something false to deceive|Keeping a secret|A joke|1|CCC 2482.',
    'What did Zacchaeus do after he met Jesus?|Ran away|Gave back what he had taken and more|Said nothing|Hid his money|1|Luke 19:8.',
    'What is the way back from a lie?|Hide it|Say what happened, say sorry, make it right|Wait|Blame someone|1|CCC 1424.',
  ],
  life='This week, when you are tempted to hide a mistake, use the three sentences: “I did it. I am sorry. I will make it right.” Try it at home first. At the end of the week, tell a parent about one time you did it.',
  saint='Francis of Assisi: a rich young man who stood up in front of the whole town and gave everything back to his father, saying he belonged to his Father in heaven.',
  misc=[
    '“A small lie is nothing.”',
    '“Cheating is okay if no one finds out.”',
    '“If I say sorry, I will always get in trouble.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:confession'],
  mem='“Let what you say be simply ‘Yes’ or ‘No.’” (Matthew 5:37)',
  assess='Ask each child to say what a lie is, explain how cheating is a kind of lie, and say the three sentences for making things right.',
  script=dict(
    open='Hide a small object in the room, act as if you cannot find it, and wait. Then say, “I lied. I hid it.” Ask, “How did it feel to watch me pretend? Why would telling the truth feel better?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The eighth commandment says, ‘You shall not bear false witness.’ It asks us to tell the truth. A lie is saying something false in order to fool someone. Lies hurt trust. Trust is like glue between friends and family. Every lie, even a small one, makes the glue weaker. Jesus said, ‘Let your yes be yes and your no be no.’”',
      'Say: “Cheating is a lie we act out. Copying an answer or breaking a game rule pretends we did something we did not. It hurts us too, because we do not learn, and it’s unfair to others. When you are honest, you can be proud of your own work, even if it is not perfect.”',
      'Say: “You have a conscience. It is a small inner alarm that says, ‘That is not right.’ It rings before and after a wrong choice. We can ignore it, but it is better to listen. We train it by learning what is right, praying, and asking wise people. The more you listen, the clearer it gets.”',
      'Say: “Zacchaeus cheated people. When Jesus came to his house, he said he would give back four times what he took. How do you fix a lie? Say what happened. Say sorry. Make it right. In Confession we can say sorry to God and get strength. Telling the truth is scary for a minute, and a relief for a long time.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What does a lie feel like inside?',
      'What is a time someone told you the truth and it helped you?',
      'What would you say if you broke something and nobody saw?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Make three scenario cards: a broken thing, copied homework, a game cheat.',
      'In groups, act the lie, then act the truth, then act the fix.',
      'Each child writes the three sentences: I did it. I am sorry. I will make it right.',
    ],
    close='Pray the act of contrition together. Say, “Truth sets you free. Say it small, say it soon.”'),
  tryit=['Act a lie, the truth, and the fix.', 'Write three sentences for making it right.', 'Pick one scene to try at home.'],
  saintStory='Francis was born around 1181 in Assisi, Italy. His father, Pietro, was a rich cloth merchant. As a young man Francis loved parties, fine clothes and stories of knights. After being a prisoner of war and suffering a long illness, he began to think about what God wanted.\nOne day, while praying in the broken church of San Damiano, he heard Jesus say from the cross, “Francis, rebuild my church.” He sold some of his father’s cloth to pay for repairs. His father was furious and took him to the bishop. In the square at Assisi, in front of the bishop and the crowd, Francis took off his fine clothes, laid them at his father’s feet, and said that from now on he would say “Our Father who art in heaven,” not “father Pietro.”\nHe went on to live in poverty with his friends, the Franciscans. He preached to people and to birds, and loved all creatures. He died in 1226, and was canonized only two years later. Francis was honest even when it cost him everything.',
),

L('Big feelings: anger, fear and sadness, and where to take them',
  'Nine- and ten-year-olds often cannot yet say what they feel, so feelings come out as stomachaches, shouting or silence. The Catechism says feelings are not good or bad in themselves, Jesus felt them all, and prayer, the Psalms, trusted grown-ups and the body’s own rhythms (sleep, movement, breath) all help. This lesson gives children names for feelings and a plan for each.',
  '1762-1770,1767,2559,2563,2288,1500',
  'Psalm 56:3-4;Psalm 4:8;Mark 14:32-36;Philippians 4:6',
  'prayer peace hope virtue',
  'What feeling is hardest for you to handle? What happens in your body when it comes?',
  'Make a “feelings toolbox” card with four feelings (angry, scared, sad, lonely). For each, write one thing your body does, one thing that helps (a breath, a walk, a hug, a song), one prayer sentence, and one person you can tell. Practise “smell the flower, blow out the candle” breathing together.',
  'guardian-angel',
  id='4-L2',
  hook='Show pictures or faces of five feelings. Ask children to act each one with their face and body. Then ask, “Which of these is Jesus allowed to feel?” Read Mark 14:34: “My soul is very sorrowful.”',
  teach=[
    'The Catechism says our feelings are part of who God made us to be, and that “in themselves passions are neither good nor evil” (CCC 1767). A feeling is like weather: it comes and goes. Anger, fear, sadness, joy, jealousy, loneliness are all normal. What matters is what we do with them. We can guide our feelings with our mind and will, with help from God and other people (CCC 1768–1770).',
    'Jesus had feelings. He was angry at people who cheated in the Temple, he wept at the tomb of his friend Lazarus, and in the garden before he died he was afraid and sad. He said, “My soul is very sorrowful” (Mark 14:34), and he asked his friends to stay near. He prayed. He did not hide what he felt. If Jesus felt these things, they are not shameful.',
    'Here is a toolbox. First, notice the feeling in your body: hot face, tight tummy, shaky legs. Second, name it: “I am angry,” “I am scared.” Third, do something calming: breathe slowly, move your body, drink water, hug someone, listen to music. Fourth, pray: you can just say, “Jesus, help me.” The Psalms are prayers for hard feelings: “When I am afraid, I put my trust in you” (Psalm 56:3). Fifth, tell a trusted grown-up. Telling makes the feeling smaller.',
    'Taking care of our bodies helps our feelings (CCC 2288). Sleep, food, exercise and fresh air all matter. If a feeling is very big or lasts a long time, or if you feel unsafe or want to hurt yourself or someone else, tell a grown-up right away. That is not weak; it is wise. God gave us people to help.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Where in your body do you feel anger? Fear? Sadness?',
    'What is something that helps you calm down?',
    'Why do you think Jesus let his friends see he was sad?',
    'Who are the grown-ups you could tell?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about feelings?|They are bad|In themselves they are neither good nor evil|They should be hidden|Only babies have them|1|CCC 1767.',
    'Did Jesus have feelings like fear and sadness?|No|Yes, in the garden he was sorrowful and afraid|Only anger|Never|1|Mark 14:33–34.',
    'What should you do if a feeling is very big or lasts a long time?|Hide it|Tell a trusted grown-up|Ignore it|Wait for years|1|CCC 2288.',
  ],
  life='Keep your feelings toolbox card where you can see it. This week, when a big feeling comes, name it out loud (“I feel angry”), use one tool, and say a short prayer. At the end of the week, tell a grown-up how it went.',
  saint='John Bosco: a boy whose father died when he was two, who grew up poor, and who turned his big feelings into games, jokes and care for other boys.',
  misc=[
    '“Good children never get angry or scared.”',
    '“If I tell someone, I am weak.”',
    '“Feelings are wrong.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:suffering'],
  mem='“When I am afraid, I put my trust in you.” (Psalm 56:3)',
  assess='Ask each child to name two feelings, tell one tool for each, and say who they would tell.',
  script=dict(
    open='Hold up cards with five faces: happy, sad, angry, scared, lonely. Ask children to make each face and show how their body feels. Then ask, “Which of these can Jesus feel?” Let them answer, then read Mark 14:34.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “God made us with feelings. The Catechism says feelings are not good or bad in themselves. They are like weather: they come and go. Angry, scared, sad, lonely, happy: all normal. What matters is what we do. We can guide our feelings with our minds, with prayer, and with help from others.”',
      'Say: “Jesus had feelings too. He was angry in the Temple. He cried at his friend Lazarus’s tomb. In the garden he was sorrowful and afraid, and he asked his friends to stay close. He prayed. He did not hide it. So if you feel these things, you are not bad. You are like Jesus.”',
      'Say: “Here is a toolbox. One: notice the feeling in your body. Two: name it. Three: do something calming: breathe, move, drink water, hug someone. Four: pray. Even ‘Jesus, help me’ is a prayer. The Psalms are full of prayers for hard feelings. Five: tell a trusted grown-up. Telling makes the feeling smaller.”',
      'Say: “Sleep, food, movement and fresh air help our feelings. If a feeling is very big, lasts a long time, or you feel unsafe or want to hurt yourself or someone else, tell a grown-up right away. That is not weak. It is wise. God gave you people who want to help.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Where do you feel anger in your body?',
      'What helps you calm down?',
      'Who are two grown-ups you could tell?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Practise “smell the flower, blow out the candle” breathing together, three times.',
      'Give each child a toolbox card with four feelings. They write one body sign, one thing that helps, one prayer sentence and one person for each.',
      'Partner up and share one line from the card.',
    ],
    close='Pray the guardian angel prayer together. Say, “Every feeling can be brought to Jesus.”'),
  tryit=['Make a feelings toolbox card.', 'Practise slow breathing.', 'Share one line with a partner.'],
  saintStory='John Bosco was born in 1815 in a small farmhouse in Becchi, Italy. When he was two, his father died, and his mother Margaret raised him and his brothers alone. They were very poor. At nine he had a dream in which he saw a crowd of rough boys, fighting and swearing, and a lady told him to win them over not with blows but with gentleness and kindness.\nJohn learned to juggle and do acrobatics and magic tricks, and would gather boys on Sundays to watch. Then he would say the rosary with them. He became a priest in 1841 and began to look after poor boys in Turin, who worked in factories and slept in the streets. He ran a place where they could play, learn a trade, pray, and be loved. He turned big feelings into good energy through games, music and jokes.\nHe founded the Salesians, who still teach children around the world. He died in 1888, and was canonized in 1934. He is the patron saint of young people.',
),

L('When someone dies: heaven, and how to be sad',
  'Many children lose a grandparent, a pet or a classmate by the age of ten. They need honest words and a real hope. The Church’s teaching on death, heaven, the communion of saints, prayer for the dead and funerals gives children something firm to hold, and permission to cry. This lesson helps children grieve without fear and care for others who are sad.',
  '1005-1014,1024-1029,1030-1032,946-962,1680-1690,2299',
  'John 11:17-44;John 14:1-3;Revelation 21:1-5;Psalm 23',
  'heaven eternal-life communion-of-saints hope',
  'Has anyone you love ever died? What do you remember about them?',
  'Make a “remembering page” for someone you have lost (or for a pet): draw them, write three things you remember, and write a prayer (“Jesus, please take care of them”). Choose one thing to do for them this week, such as lighting a candle or saying a prayer at bedtime.',
  'eternal-rest',
  id='4-L3',
  hook='Read John 11:35, “Jesus wept.” Ask: “Why did Jesus cry when he knew he would soon bring Lazarus back? What does that tell us about being sad?”',
  teach=[
    'Death is sad. Jesus himself cried when his friend Lazarus died (John 11:35). It is normal to miss people, to cry, to feel angry or confused. Tears are a way love comes out. The Church says there is no shame in grieving. Saint Paul says we do not grieve “as others do who have no hope” (1 Thessalonians 4:13).',
    'Here is our hope. For Christians, death is not the end. When a person dies, their soul goes to God, and their body will rise on the last day, because Jesus rose from the dead (CCC 1005–1014). Jesus said, “In my Father’s house are many rooms... I go to prepare a place for you” (John 14:2). Heaven is being with God and with everyone we love who has died in his friendship. The Catechism says it is perfect happiness, life with the Trinity (CCC 1024–1026).',
    'We are still connected to those who have died. The Church calls this the communion of saints: everyone who belongs to Jesus, in heaven, on earth and being made ready, is one family (CCC 946–962). We pray for them, and they pray for us. We say, “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.” We can offer Mass for them, light candles, and do kind things in their memory (CCC 1032). That gives our sadness something to do.',
    'When someone dies, the Church holds a funeral to comfort the family and pray for the person who died (CCC 1680–1690). If you are sad, you can tell a grown-up, draw a picture, look at photos, or pray. You can also help others who are sad by sitting with them, making a card, or just being kind. If your sadness feels very heavy or does not go away, ask a grown-up for help.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is something you remember about someone you have lost?',
    'Why do you think Jesus cried?',
    'What does it mean to pray for someone who has died?',
    'How can you help a friend who is sad?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Is it wrong to cry when someone dies?|Yes|No, Jesus wept at the death of his friend|Only babies cry|Only at funerals|1|John 11:35.',
    'What does the Church teach about heaven?|It is a myth|It is life with God, perfect happiness|It is only for adults|It is far away|1|CCC 1024–1026.',
    'What can we do for those who have died?|Nothing|Pray for them and offer Mass|Forget them|Only visit graves|1|CCC 1032.',
  ],
  life='This week, say “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord” at bedtime for one person you have lost. Make a card or do a kind act in their memory. And notice someone who is sad, and do one kind thing for them.',
  saint='Jacinta Marto: a Portuguese girl of nine who prayed for sinners and offered her sufferings, and who died of illness at nine, believing she would soon be with Mary.',
  misc=[
    '“If I am sad, I do not have faith.”',
    '“The person who died is gone and cannot be helped.”',
    '“Heaven is only clouds.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:heaven', 'apol:praying-for-the-dead'],
  mem='“Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.”',
  assess='Ask each child to say what the Church teaches about heaven, name one thing they can do for someone who has died, and one thing they can do for someone who is sad.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read John 11:35 slowly, “Jesus wept.” Ask, “Why do you think Jesus cried? He knew he would soon bring Lazarus back.” Let children answer. Say, “Crying is a way love comes out.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Death is sad. Jesus cried when his friend died. It is normal to miss people, to cry, and even to feel angry or confused. St. Paul says we do not grieve like people who have no hope. We are allowed to be sad, and we also have a hope.”',
      'Say: “Here is our hope. For Christians, death is not the end. When someone dies, their soul goes to God, and one day their body will rise, because Jesus rose. Jesus said, ‘In my Father’s house are many rooms. I am going to prepare a place for you.’ Heaven is being with God and with everyone we love who has died in his friendship.”',
      'Say: “We are still connected. The Church calls it the communion of saints: everyone who belongs to Jesus is one family. We pray for them and they pray for us. We say, ‘Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.’ We can offer a Mass, light a candle, or do a kind thing in their memory. That gives our sadness something to do.”',
      'Say: “When someone dies, the Church has a funeral to comfort the family and pray for the one who died. If you are sad you can tell a grown-up, draw a picture, look at photos, or pray. And you can help others: sit with someone who is sad, make a card, be kind. If the sadness feels very heavy, ask a grown-up for help.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is a happy memory you have of someone who has died?',
      'What do you want to say to them?',
      'What could you do for a friend who is sad?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out paper. Children draw someone they miss, write three memories, and a prayer sentence.',
      'Light a single candle in the middle (or an LED) and say the eternal rest prayer together.',
      'Each child chooses one thing to do this week for that person or for someone who is sad.',
    ],
    close='Pray the eternal rest together. Say, “Love does not end. Pray for them, and be kind to people who are sad.”'),
  tryit=['Draw someone you miss.', 'Write three memories and a prayer.', 'Choose one kind act this week.'],
  saintStory='Jacinta Marto was born in 1910 in the village of Aljustrel, in Portugal, the youngest of the children who saw the Virgin Mary at Fatima. She lived with her family of shepherds, and she and her brother Francisco and cousin Lucia looked after the sheep. She was a lively, stubborn, cheerful little girl who loved to dance and sing.\nIn 1917, when she was seven, the three children saw Our Lady in the fields near Fatima six times, from May to October. Mary asked them to pray the rosary and make sacrifices for sinners. Jacinta took it very seriously. She gave up her lunch for the poor, wore a rope around her waist as a penance, and prayed for people she did not know. She was sometimes seen praying with her face pressed to the ground.\nIn 1918, a terrible flu epidemic struck. Francisco died in April 1919 at ten. Jacinta, who was very ill, was moved to a hospital in Lisbon, and died on February 20, 1920, at nine. She said that Mary had told her she would soon come to take her. In 2017 she and Francisco were canonized, the youngest non-martyr saints in the Church.',
),
])
